Make pod startup an owned cancellable task

Startup workers could retain lifecycle locks after their clients left,
blocking recovery from stopped containers and stalled setup commands.

Own startup, reconnect, fork, and recreate as async tasks. Delete aborts
active and queued startup tasks, waits for their destruction, then takes
the lifecycle lock and removes remaining pod state. Expose pending
startup before container creation so delete can also cancel initializers
and image builds. Client disconnection detaches from startup.

Await subprocesses and network operations throughout startup while
keeping filesystem and database work synchronous. Resource guards kill
cancelled process groups and release temporary files, connections,
agents, and proxy sessions. Backend cleanup uses the reserved pod name
when a create request acted before its reply was lost.

Bound readiness connection attempts and idle reads. Startup heartbeats
allow healthy lifecycle commands to run without an overall deadline.
Fail an established readiness stream when its container disappears.

Regression tests cover abandoned readiness waits, stopped and paused
containers, queued enters, initializer and builder descendants, host Git
fetches, failed-start cleanup, and cancellation after container
creation. Transport tests preserve half-closed SSH responses and partial
tunnel frames during concurrent events.

Validation: required smoke and feature filters passed via cargo
pipeline. The full suite and its resumed checks passed 596 tests with
two skips. macOS validation was unavailable because the macos host did
not resolve.
